---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="Methodology — Petty Evals">
  <article class="prose">
    <p style="color: var(--pe-muted);" class="text-sm uppercase tracking-widest">Methodology</p>
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-semibold mt-2 mb-6">How Petty Evals measures what models actually do</h1>

    <p>
      Petty Evals is a benchmark suite for stylistic and formatting choices in code
      generation. Every fight evaluates a single decision — tabs vs spaces, single
      vs double quotes, <code>===</code> vs <code>==</code> — and reports a verdict
      across a fixed panel of frontier language models. This page describes the
      protocol used for every fight.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-3">Run protocol</h2>
    <p>
      For each fight we issue, per model and per temperature setting,
      <strong>N = 10 runs</strong> against each of <strong>3 prompt variants</strong>.
      We sweep two temperatures: <code>0.0</code> for deterministic decoding and
      <code>0.7</code> as a representative default. The system prompt is empty;
      we test the bare model with no priming.
    </p>
    <p>
      The result for one (fight × model) cell is therefore 60 generations. The
      headline vote is the modal classification across those 60 outputs.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-3">Classification</h2>
    <p>
      The primary classifier for each fight is deterministic — a regular
      expression or small AST inspection that maps an output to a label like
      <code>tabs</code>, <code>spaces</code>, or <code>unknown</code>. When the
      primary classifier returns <code>unknown</code>, an LLM judge (Claude Haiku,
      temperature 0) is consulted as a tie-breaker. Inter-rater reliability
      against a 30-output hand-labeled subsample is reported as
      Cohen's <em>kappa</em>; fights with kappa below 0.7 are flagged and do not
      receive a verdict.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-3">Statistics</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1">
      <li>
        <strong>Confidence interval:</strong> Wilson score 95% CI on the modal
        proportion. Chosen over Wald because the Wald interval misbehaves at
        small <em>n</em> and at proportions near 0 or 1.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Verdict:</strong> majority vote across models. Each model gets
        one vote. Ties are reported as ties; we do not apply a tiebreaker.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Disagreement Index:</strong> normalized Shannon entropy of the
        per-model vote distribution. 0 = unanimous, 1 = maximum chaos. The
        homepage leaderboard sorts by this value.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-3">Reproducibility</h2>
    <p>
      Every prompt and classifier lives in the source repository, committed
      before the run. Every result file is committed alongside the prompts that
      produced it. Anyone with API keys for the four configured providers can
      reproduce a fight with one command:
    </p>
    <pre class="bg-white border p-3 overflow-x-auto" style="border-color: var(--pe-rule);"><code>pnpm evals --fight=tabs-vs-spaces-python</code></pre>

    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-3">Cost and provenance</h2>
    <p>
      Each fight page records the wall-clock start and finish of its run, the
      total token count, and the total cost in USD computed from per-provider
      published prices at run time. Model identifiers are pinned. When a model
      version changes, the fight is re-run and the prior result remains in git
      history.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
